[0.000, "o", "s or builds won't die if the laptop closes.                                                                                                                \u001b[0m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[6;1H\u001b[2K \u001b[38;2;189;127;231m- \u001b[39mAnything I write to the filesystem (e.g., under \u001b[38;2;189;127;231m/workspace\u001b[39m) persists between interactions.                                                                                     \u001b[0m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[7;1H\u001b[2K \u001b[38;2;189;127;231m- \u001b[39mI don't have memory across sessions beyond what's in files, so if you want something remembered, I should write it down.                                                       \u001b[0m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[8;1H\u001b[2K                                                                                                                                                                                  \u001b[0m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[9;1H\u001b[2K Want me to do anything with that in mind — like kick off a long task, or set up notes/state files for continuity?                                                                \u001b[0"]
[0.000, "o", "m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[10;1H\u001b[2K \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;151;151;151mThe user is telling me they're running in a sandbox, and their user can close their computer and this (the sandbox/agent) will keep running. They seem to be making a statement, \u001b[0m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[11;1H\u001b[2K \u001b[3;38;2;151;151;151mperhaps wanting confirmation or discussion about what that means. There's no explicit question. I should respond concisely, acknowledging the setup: the sandbox persists        \u001b[0m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[12;1H\u001b[2K \u001b[3;38;2;151;151;151mindependently of the local machine, so long-running tasks can continue, sessions can be resumed, etc. Maybe note implications: I can run long commands, but I don't have         \u001b[0m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[13;1H\u001b[2K \u001b[3;38;2;151;151;151mpersistent memory between sessions unless files are written; state persists in the filesystem of the sandbox. Keep it brief and conversational.\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                  \u001b[0m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[16;1H\u001b[2K\u001b[48;2;0;51;70m \u001b[39mcreate a file called stream.txt and wrie to it \"hello world \" + timestamp every 3 seconds. ru"]
[0.000, "o", "n the writes to this file as a background job                                       \u001b[49m\u001b[0m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[19;1H\u001b[2K \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;151;151;151mThe user wants a background job that appends \"hello world \" + timestamp to stream.txt every 3 seconds. I should create the file and start a background loop with nohup or        \u001b[0m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[20;1H\u001b[2K \u001b[3;38;2;151;151;151msimilar so it keeps running after the bash tool call returns.\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                                                                    \u001b[0m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[21;1H\u001b[2K                                                                                                                                                                                  \u001b[0m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[22;1H\u001b[2K \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;151;151;151mI'll use a bash command with nohup and & to detach it. Something like:\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                                                           \u001b[0m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[23;1H\u001b[2K                               "]
